Skip decode blocks the sliding-window mask would zero anyway.

vllm/v1/attention/ops/chunked_prefill_paged_decode.py iterates every block of the
sequence and then masks everything outside the window to -10000. The mask keeps a
token when (context_len - seq_offset) < SLIDING_WINDOW with context_len =
seq_len - 1, so the lowest surviving absolute index is seq_len - SLIDING_WINDOW
and every block below it is masked in full. exp(-10000 - m) is zero, so starting
the loop there is an identity, not an approximation.

Measured on Muse-Glimmer-30B, gfx1100, TP=2, 32768 context, 2048 window,
block_size 16: 2048 blocks read where 128 are needed. 1.589 ms -> 154.796 us per
kernel call, 83.99 -> 26.63 ms per generated token (3.15x), and 2.75x on
gemma-3-27b, against 1.00x below each model's own window. Correctness: upstream's
own tests/kernels/attention/test_prefix_prefill.py passes with no case changing
outcome, and 15 boundary cases are bit-identical under torch.equal. See
docs/sliding-window-block-skip.md; gemma-4 is routed to a different backend and
is unaffected.

Against vLLM 0.23.1.dev1+g9ddef7117. main as of 2026-08-24 has the same loop.

